edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555769 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555769 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cf3361e-986e-5750-bf9f-73762dff3b0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555769 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555795 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555795 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555795 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555795 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42d466fd-6c03-513d-b916-3193ae5d935b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555795 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=7,{u}_{22}=-5,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-35.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555807 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555807 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555807 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555807 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55b8ab85-3682-5003-bc82-d3e9f5248d03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555807 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555856 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555856 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555856 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555856 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555856 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d20ab7a7-2299-5eca-8c7e-9709ddaf034c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555856 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
